1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,712
Aperture Desk Job puts a control panel and
button prompts inside the scene.

2
00:00:04,712 --> 00:00:09,502
Half-Life: Alyx highlights objects you can
pick up as the hand moves.

3
00:00:09,502 --> 00:00:14,032
Even with these cues, a press followed by no
visible change raises another question.

4
00:00:14,032 --> 00:00:17,882
Was the input missed, or received but
unavailable in this state?

5
00:00:17,882 --> 00:00:20,692
Let us try to open a door in an original test.

6
00:00:20,692 --> 00:00:24,092
Both doors are locked, and both panels receive
the same button press.

7
00:00:24,092 --> 00:00:27,952
One stays silent; the other briefly explains
why the door is locked.

8
00:00:27,952 --> 00:00:32,320
Acknowledging an input is different from
praising a successful action.

9
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:36,502
This test lets us choose a spot for a
building.

10
00:00:36,502 --> 00:00:40,992
Both panels refuse to build on a space that is
already occupied.

11
00:00:40,992 --> 00:00:45,462
One changes color; the other also explains
that another building is in the way.

12
00:00:45,462 --> 00:00:49,602
On an empty space, insufficient materials
produce a different message.

13
00:00:49,602 --> 00:00:53,552
A different reason suggests a different next
action.

14
00:00:53,552 --> 00:00:57,242
We avoid using one vague unavailable label for
every condition.

15
00:00:57,242 --> 00:01:01,492
Keep the explanation brief and state the
reason in words as well as color.

16
00:01:01,492 --> 00:01:05,680
The quantities and layout in this test are
original teaching conditions.

17
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:10,432
A player might press a different confirmation
key in the start menu.

18
00:01:10,432 --> 00:01:15,602
One panel accepts Enter; the other accepts
Enter and Space.

19
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:19,773
The second panel displays the keys it accepts
beside the menu.

20
00:01:19,773 --> 00:01:23,622
This explores mapping familiar confirmation
keys to the same intention.

21
00:01:23,622 --> 00:01:28,363
Do not mix in buttons whose results differ,
such as Back or Delete.

22
00:01:28,363 --> 00:01:32,242
Accepting more keys is not always the right
choice.

23
00:01:32,242 --> 00:01:35,702
First check for conflicts with your game’s
control conventions.

24
00:01:35,702 --> 00:01:40,080
Make the accepted input clear, then show the
resulting state.

25
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,413
A press intended to skip a cutscene can also
seem to do nothing.

26
00:01:45,413 --> 00:01:49,303
In this test, a short press reveals the skip
prompt.

27
00:01:49,303 --> 00:01:53,753
Holding the button fills an indicator; only a
full hold skips the scene.

28
00:01:53,753 --> 00:01:58,363
This choice protects against losing the entire
scene after one accidental tap.

29
00:01:58,363 --> 00:02:03,432
The game acknowledges the intention before
asking for commitment.

30
00:02:03,432 --> 00:02:07,363
Release the button early to cancel
confirmation while the scene continues.

31
00:02:07,363 --> 00:02:10,893
A hold is not required for every cutscene.

32
00:02:10,893 --> 00:02:15,680
Distinguish an apparently missed press from a
press that requires a wait.

33
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:19,813
Some actions, such as saving, do not finish
immediately.

34
00:02:19,813 --> 00:02:24,473
This test first displays Saving when it
receives a request.

35
00:02:24,473 --> 00:02:28,143
It does not say Saved while the operation is
still pending.

36
00:02:28,143 --> 00:02:33,363
Another press during processing reports
progress instead of queuing more requests.

37
00:02:33,363 --> 00:02:36,213
The label changes only after completion is
confirmed.

38
00:02:36,213 --> 00:02:40,953
A game with real persistence also needs a
response when saving fails.

39
00:02:40,953 --> 00:02:45,013
Here, a simulated delayed operation
illustrates the state transitions.

40
00:02:45,013 --> 00:02:49,760
Received, pending, and complete explain what
the wait means.

41
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:54,643
A message and sound for every key can quickly
clutter the screen.

42
00:02:54,643 --> 00:02:59,533
This test responds to interaction during play
and to confirmation in a menu.

43
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:03,703
It does not attach a large warning to keys
with no meaning in the current state.

44
00:03:03,703 --> 00:03:08,563
Prioritize reasons when an action that appears
available is blocked.

45
00:03:08,563 --> 00:03:13,403
Check whether the input arrived, why the
action is unavailable, and what to try next.

46
00:03:13,403 --> 00:03:17,263
A response does not need to make every
attempted action succeed.

47
00:03:17,263 --> 00:03:20,873
It should make success, refusal, and waiting
understandable.

48
00:03:20,873 --> 00:03:24,880
Start with moments in your game where a player
feels that nothing happened.
